<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-from-pseudometrics-to-metrics" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    From pseudometrics to metrics
  </title>
  
  <idx>
    pseudometric
  </idx>
  
  <idx>
    pseudometric space
  </idx>
  
  <introduction>
    <p>
      A real-valued function <m> \rho \colon X^{ 2 } \to \R </m> on the Cartesian square <m> X^{ 2 } </m> of a set <m> X </m>, which is called the <term> ground set </term> and whose elements are called <term> points </term>, is a <term> pseudometric </term> on <m> X </m> if it has the following properties:
      
      <ol>
        <li>
          <title>
            Identity
          </title>
          
          <p>
            <m> \rho \of{ x , x } = 0 </m> for any point <m> x \in X </m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        
        <li>
          <title>
            Symmetry
          </title>
          
          <p>
            <m> \rho \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } = \rho \of{ x_{ 2 } , x_{ 1 } } </m> for any points <m> x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } \in X </m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        
        <li>
          <title>
            Triangle inequality
          </title>
          
          <p>
            <m> \rho \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 3 } } \leq \rho \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } + \rho \of{ x_{ 2 } , x_{ 3 } } </m> for any points <m> x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } , x_{ 3 } \in X </m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      
      In this case, the ordered pair <m> \of{ X , \rho } </m> is called a <term> pseudometric space </term>.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> \rho </m> be a pseudometric on a set <m> X </m>, and consider the binary relation <m> \equiv_{ \rho } </m> on <m> X </m> defined so that <m> x_{ 1 } \equiv_{ \rho } x_{ 2 } </m> if and only if <m> \rho \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } = 0 </m>. Prove that <m> \equiv_{ \rho } </m> is an equivalence relation on <m> X </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <answer>
      <p>
        asdf
      </p>
    </answer>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        asdf
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove that a pseudometric <m> \rho </m> descends to a well-defined metric <m> \overline{ \rho } </m> on the quotient set <m> X / { \equiv_{ \rho } } </m> defined by the formula
        
        <md>
          \overline{ \rho } \of{ \class{ x_{ 1 } }_{ \equiv_{ \rho } } , \class{ x_{ 2 } }_{ \equiv_{ \rho } } } = \rho \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } }
        </md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <answer>
      <p>
        asdf
      </p>
    </answer>
    
    <solution>
      <p>
        asdf
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
</exercise>
